[require oo::dialect [opt 0.3.4]]
[description]
[para]

The [package oo::dialect] package creates [term dialects]: variants of
[cmd oo::class] and [cmd oo::define] whose class definition scripts
understand additional keywords. A dialect is a namespace containing

[list_begin itemized]
[item] a metaclass, [var name][cmd ::class], whose instances are the
classes of the dialect,
[item] a common base class, [var name][cmd ::object], from which every
class of the dialect inherits,
[item] a command [var name][cmd ::define], the counterpart of
[cmd oo::define],
[item] a namespace [var name][const ::define] holding the keywords of the
dialect. Initially it contains the keywords of [cmd oo::define] and a few
more, described in section [sectref Keywords]. New keywords are added by
creating commands in this namespace.
[list_end]

A dialect can be derived from another dialect, inheriting its keywords and
its base class.

[section Commands]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd ::oo::dialect::create] [arg name] [opt [arg parent]]]

Creates the dialect [arg name]. A relative [arg name] is resolved in the
namespace of the caller. Without [arg parent], the dialect starts with the
keywords of [cmd oo::define] and its class [var name][cmd ::object]
inherits from [cmd oo::object].

[para]

With [arg parent], the name of an existing dialect (resolved the same
way), the new dialect starts with all keywords of [arg parent] and its
[term {dynamic methods}] hook (see below), and [var name][cmd ::object]
inherits from [arg parent][cmd ::object]. Keywords added to [arg parent]
later are not seen by the new dialect, so a derived dialect should be
created after its parent is complete.

[call [var name][cmd ::class] [method create] [arg class] [opt [arg script]]]

Creates the class [arg class] of the dialect and returns its fully
qualified name. The class inherits from [var name][cmd ::object]. The
definition [arg script] is evaluated like one given to
[var name][cmd ::define].

[call [var name][cmd ::define] [arg class] [arg script]]
[call [var name][cmd ::define] [arg class] [arg keyword] [opt [arg "arg ..."]]]

Like [cmd oo::define], evaluates the definition [arg script] for
[arg class], or applies a single [arg keyword] with its arguments, using
the keywords of the dialect. A relative [arg class] name is resolved in the
namespace of the caller. If [arg class] does not exist yet, it is created
as by [var name][cmd ::class] [method create], and its fully qualified name
is returned. This allows the same command to be used for creating a class
and extending it later, for example when a library is reloaded.

[para]

The script is evaluated in the namespace [var name][const ::define], so
commands not found there are looked up in the global namespace.

[call [var name][cmd ::dynamic_methods] [arg class]]

A hook called with the fully qualified name of [arg class] each time
[var name][cmd ::define] or [var name][cmd ::class] [method create] has
applied a definition to the class (when a class is created, this happens
more than once). By default it does nothing. A dialect can redefine it with
[cmd proc] to generate methods from what the keywords have recorded.

[list_end]

[section Keywords]

In addition to the keywords of [cmd oo::define] that exist when the
dialect is created (each applied to the class being defined), definition
scripts of a dialect understand:

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd current_class]]

Returns the fully qualified name of the class being defined. This is
what the procedures implementing new keywords use to find their class.

[call [cmd superclass] [opt [arg "class ..."]]]

Sets the superclasses of the class being defined, as [cmd oo::define]
does, but always keeps [var name][cmd ::object] as the last superclass
(unless the class being defined is [var name][cmd ::object] itself).
Duplicate classes are removed. Each [arg class] may be an alias declared
with [cmd aliases]. Other names are looked up first in the namespace of the
class being defined, then in the global namespace.

[call [cmd aliases] [opt [arg "name ..."]]]

Declares additional names under which the class being defined can be
given to [cmd superclass]. An alias is not a command. Within the dialect
the alias can be used by its plain name; to other dialects it is known as
a name in the namespace of the class. An alias belongs to the first class
that declares it.

[list_end]

A new keyword is a command, usually a [cmd proc], created in the namespace
[var name][const ::define]. It is called with the arguments given in the
definition script and uses [cmd current_class] to find the class:

[example {
proc ::mydialect::define::option {name default} {
    oo::define [current_class] method $name {} [list return $default]
}
}]

Defining a keyword with the name of an existing one, for example
[cmd constructor], replaces it for this dialect and for dialects derived
from it later.

[section {Class methods}]

Classes of a dialect have one additional method:

[list_begin definitions]

[call [arg class] [method aliases]]

Returns the fully qualified aliases declared for [arg class] with the
keyword [cmd aliases] and not already taken by another class.

[list_end]

[section Example]

[example {
package require oo::dialect
oo::dialect::create shape

# A new keyword
proc ::shape::define::sides {n} {
    oo::define [current_class] method sides {} [list return $n]
}

# Behaviour shared by all classes of the dialect
::shape::define ::shape::object {
    method describe {} {
        return "[info object class [self]] has [my sides] sides"
    }
}

shape::class create polygon {
    aliases Polygon
    sides 0
}
shape::define square {
    superclass Polygon
    sides 4
}

puts [info class superclasses square]
# -> ::polygon ::shape::object
puts [[square new] describe]
# -> ::square has 4 sides

# A dialect derived from shape
oo::dialect::create solid shape

proc ::solid::define::faces {n} {
    oo::define [current_class] method faces {} [list return $n]
}

solid::define cube {
    sides 4
    faces 6
}
puts [info class superclasses solid::object]
# -> ::shape::object
puts [[cube new] faces]
# -> 6
}]

